Part 2: How to Make a Lightweight ROS 2 Mars Yard Map for Simulation
To make the map simulation-friendly and lightweight, you should choose the method based on your target ROS 2 components:

Approach A: Use Gazebo Heightmaps (Highly Recommended for Physics & Performance)
Instead of loading the heavy 246 MB .dae file, configure Gazebo to render the terrain directly from the grayscale heightmap image (map.png in heightMap/).

Prepare the Files: Move map.png into your ROS 2 package's assets (e.g., src/my_mars_simulation/worlds/textures/).
Configure the SDF: Create/edit a .sdf world file. Replace the <mesh> tag with a <heightmap> tag:
xml
<model name="mars_yard_heightmap">
  <static>true</static>
  <link name="link">
    <collision name="collision">
      <geometry>
        <heightmap>
          <uri>model://my_mars_simulation/worlds/textures/map.png</uri>
          <size>30 30 2</size> <!-- Scale: Width (m), Length (m), Max Height (m) -->
          <pos>0 0 0</pos>
        </heightmap>
      </geometry>
    </collision>
    <visual name="visual">
      <geometry>
        <heightmap>
          <use_terrain_paging>false</use_terrain_paging>
          <texture>
            <diffuse>model://my_mars_simulation/worlds/textures/HiRes_Ortofotomapa_Mars2025_PL2000.png</diffuse>
            <normal>model://flat_normal.png</normal>
            <size>30</size>
          </texture>
          <uri>model://my_mars_simulation/worlds/textures/map.png</uri>
          <size>30 30 2</size>
          <pos>0 0 0</pos>
        </heightmap>
      </geometry>
    </visual>
  </link>
</model>
Why this works: Gazebo handles heightmap images exponentially faster than mesh geometry, saving system memory and CPU cycles.
Approach B: Decimate and Bake the Mesh in Blender
If you need exact overhangs, craters, or features that a 2.5D heightmap cannot model, optimize the Blender file:

Open SecondTry.blend in Blender.
Select the mesh object (NewyardMesh or similar).
Add a Decimate Modifier and set the Ratio to 0.05 or 0.10 (reducing polygon count by 90-95%).
Select Bake to map details from the high-res texture and normal map onto the low-poly mesh.
Export the file as mars_yard_optimized.dae (which should now be < 15 MB).
Point your model.sdf file's <uri> tag to the newly generated mars_yard_optimized.dae instead of the original heavy mesh.
Approach C: Generate a 2D Occupancy Grid for Nav2
If you intend to run navigation (Nav2) on this map, you will need a 2D occupancy grid (.pgm image + .yaml description). There are two ways to do this:

Option C1 (From Simulation mapping):

Spawn your optimized 3D model/heightmap in Gazebo.
Spawn your robot (e.g. Panther UGV) with its sensor pipeline active (LiDAR or depth-to-laser).
Run a SLAM package:
bash
ros2 launch slam_toolbox online_async_launch.py
Drive the robot around using keyboard teleop to map the yard.
Save the map using the Nav2 map saver:
bash
